Uptime Kuma integration
-----------------------
Add a public /api/health endpoint (no auth) that pings all installed MCP instances.
Returns 200 + JSON status for the overall gateway, 503 if any instance is down.
Per-instance: /api/health?id=portainer-prod → 200 or 503.

The problem with the simple HTTP approach: user has to manually add one Uptime Kuma monitor
per MCP instance, which doesn't scale.

Better approach to investigate:
- MCPetty auto-generates the Uptime Kuma monitor config (bulk import via Uptime Kuma's
  backup/import JSON format) so the user just imports one file and gets all monitors at once.
- Or: MCPetty calls the Uptime Kuma API directly to create/delete monitors when instances
  are installed/uninstalled. Uptime Kuma has an undocumented but stable REST API + socket.io API.
  Credential needed: Uptime Kuma URL + username/password or API key.
  This would make monitoring fully automatic — install a Portainer instance, monitor appears.


Progressive disclosure of the action schema
-------------------------------------------
The tool schema is re-sent on EVERY request in a conversation, not once per session, and
per INSTANCE, not per type — two Proxmox instances pay for it twice. Prompt caching makes
it ~6x cheaper (cache reads are ~0.1x input) but does NOT make it smaller: it occupies the
context window on every request regardless.

Measured cost of what buildPlatformTool() emits today, all 7 native types, one instance
each (same length/4 heuristic logSchemaTokens uses):

  platform      actions   schema tokens
  wazuh              57           3,136
  firefly            28           2,492
  proxmox            38           2,449
  portainer          39           1,889
  wikijs             18             916
  karakeep           11             896
  mcpetty             6             550
  ALL               197          12,328

Split by component (sums to 11,217; the rest is JSON scaffolding, nocache, platform
descriptions, and the get_page/check_approval lines):

  action name enum (all 197)      971 tokens   <- keeps actions CALLABLE
  typed arg properties (union)  2,783 tokens   <- keeps actions CALLABLE
  per-action description lines  7,463 tokens   <- just documentation

So 67% of the cost is documentation. Reachability costs only 3,754 tokens.

THE IDEA: defer descriptions, never disable actions.
- Every action stays in the `action` enum -> always callable, nothing to re-enable.
- Every arg keeps its typed property + description -> satisfies the CLAUDE.md schema rule,
  and the model already knows what agent_id/vmid expect, so cold actions are often callable
  with no lookup at all.
- Only description lines are deferred: full text for actions actually used, plus a
  `describe_action(name)` meta-action for the rest.
- 12,328 -> roughly 5,000 with zero loss of capability.

WHY NOT the obvious "dead tool reaper" (disable actions never called in 30 days):
it's self-fulfilling. A disabled action can never be called, so it never accrues usage, so
it stays disabled forever — and the agent doesn't know it's missing, it just quietly can't
do the thing. tool_call_log should RANK what stays documented, not act as a kill list.

Tunable middle tier worth measuring: keep a one-line description for cold actions and drop
only the `[args: ...]` signature block. Lands somewhere between 3,754 and 11,217.

For actions genuinely disabled on purpose (destructive ones on a read-only key), two things
keep it non-silent:
- One line in the tool description: "12 actions are disabled on this instance." The agent
  then reports the gap instead of inventing a workaround.
- A `request_action(name, reason)` meta-action filing into the EXISTING approval queue ->
  human approves in the dashboard -> filter flips -> notifications/tools/list_changed ->
  client re-lists. Reuses approvals + tool filters + sse-bus + list_changed.

CAUTION: tools render at position 0 of the API request, so any change to the tool set
invalidates the ENTIRE prompt cache, not just the tools portion. That makes list_changed
right for a deliberate human-approved unlock and wrong for anything automatic — which is
why health flips call invalidatePlatformProbe() WITHOUT broadcasting list_changed.
